High-performance electrocatalysts for the oxygen reduction reaction are indispensable in many electrochemical energy storage and conversion technologies. However, the lack of efficient and inexpensive catalysts or catalyst systems that can compete with noble metal catalysts hinders their large-scale industrial applications. As an important class of porous materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with systematically tailored structures and compositions have recently been suggested as promising precursors to prepare diverse functional materials. Here we report a dual-MOFs confined-pyrolysis approach for preparing iron carbide nanoparticles-embedded carbon nanotube assemblies. Starting from a novel MOF-in-MOF precursor consisting of a Zn-based MOF polyhedron host and many engulfed Fe-based MOF nanorods, a complex structured composite material constructed from iron carbide nanocrystallites-embedded carbon nanotubes encapsulated in a porous carbon matrix is successfully prepared. We further demonstrate that the as-derived composite material manifests remarkable electrocatalytic performance for oxygen reduction reaction in alkaline electrolyte. The present strategy significantly expands the toolbox for design and synthesis of MOFs-derived functional materials for a wide range of applications.
The increasing global energy consumption has promoted intense research on alternative energy conversion and storage systems. In this important field, electrochemical systems, such as fuel cells and batteries provide a bridge for efficient interconversion of chemical to electrical energy.1-4 Among these electrochemical devices, fuel cells5 and metal-air batteries6, 7 based on oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) at the cathode require efficient electrocatalysts because the electrode reactions involve complex processes and show strong dependence on the composition and structure of the electrode materials. Up to now, platinum (Pt) is considered as the most active catalyst for ORR.8 However, the commercialization of fuel cells and metal-air batteries is hindered by the high cost of Pt. Synthesis of efficient and lowcost electrocatalysts that can compete with Pt remains a great challenge. Transition metal carbides, such as iron (Fe), molybdenum (Mo), and tungsten (W) carbides have recently been considered as promising catalysts for different electrocatalytic reactions.9-12 In particular, iron carbide has been demonstrated as a highly active nonprecious metal catalyst for ORR.13-17 Because the catalytic activity has close relation with the structure of electrode material, the electrocatalytic performance could be further improved by constructing proper nanostructures. Considering classical inorganic and biological catalysts, both efficient inorganic electrocatalysts and enzymes for ORR contain sufficiently separated inorganic active sites and efficient electron donating conductive frameworks or groups,18-21 which effectively prevents site overlap or catalytic deactivation and promotes the formation and release of reduced oxygen species. However, controllable synthesis of nanostructured metal carbides with sufficiently separated small active sites and protective porous conductive matrix towards high electrocatalytic activity still remains as an unsolved challenge, because of the difficulty to achieve homogeneous carburization and the inevitable aggregation of nanoparticles at high reaction temperature.
In recent years, MOFs have become an important class of porous materials with a wide range of applications in storage of gas fuels, capture of carbon dioxide, catalysis, and drug delivery.22-27 Moreover, synthesis of metal/carbon-based functional materials derived from MOFs precursors has also drawn great attention from researchers worldwide.28-31 Although a large number of MOFs has been reported covering a wide range of metal species and organic ligands, conventional MOFs-derived syntheses are mainly based on a handful of simple MOFs that can be easily prepared. Complex designed precursors with two or more different MOFs in one particle are only limited to very few core-shell structured MOFs composites which are usually prepared through epitaxial growth of different MOFs with similar crystal structure and chemical functionality.32, 33 Therefore, delicate design and synthesis of composite MOFs precursors with novel structures and tailored compositions may significantly expand the toolbox for construction of desirable metal/carbon-based functional materials.
In this work, we develop a dual-MOFs confined-pyrolysis strategy for preparation of iron carbide nanoparticlesembedded N-doped carbon nanotube assemblies supported by a porous N-doped carbon matrix (denoted as $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies) as efficient electrocatalysts for ORR. Distinct from previous studies, the present synthesis strategy relies on confined carburization of Fe-based MIL-88B nanorods embedded in Zn-based ZIF-8 polyhedron (Fig. 1). The introduction of the second MOF into a MOF host as coprecursor guarantees in situ homogeneously confined pyrolysis of MIL-88B nanorods and easy synthesis of small $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites-embedded N-CNTs, which cannot be obtained by direct carburization of sole MIL-88B. In addition, the carburization process confined within the carbon matrix derived from organic ligands can effectively prevent the aggregation of formed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites. Benefiting from the small size of these $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites and the robust and porous N-doped carbon matrix, the as-prepared $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies exhibit remarkable electrocatalytic activity for ORR in basic electrolyte.
