t (Fig. S5, see ESI†). The number of MIL-88B nanorods embedded in each ZIF8 crystal and the embedded volume of each nanorod can be controlled by tuning the aspect ratio and amount of MIL-88B nanorods added in the reaction system (Fig. S6, see ESI†).
The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies are synthesized through thermal treatment of MIL-88B $@$ ZIF-8 dual-MOFs at 900 $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ under nitrogen atmosphere. Fig. 3a shows the FESEM image of the as-obtained $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies at low magnification. The sample retains its morphology after the thermal treatment. The sizes of the rhombic dodecahedral hosts and rod-shaped guests are reduced because of the shrinkage during the annealing process, meanwhile the surfaces of the formed particles become very rough (Fig. 3 b and c). The structure of as-derived $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies is further examined by TEM as depicted in Fig. 3d. The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites-embedded N-CNTs and their poorly shaped carbon hosts are derived from MIL-88B nanorods and ZIF-8 crystals, respectively. A close examination on a $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assembly reveals the highly dispersed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanoparticles throughout the whole particle (Fig. 3e). Numerous small $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites are confined in N-CNTs (Fig. 3f). Different from these resultant $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies with well-defined overall morphology and structure, a mixture of strongly aggregated large $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ particles and empty N-CNTs is produced by direct pyrolysis of MIL-88B nanorods, while the same pyrolysis of ZIF-8 leads to formation of cracked porous carbon nanoshells (Fig. S7, see ESI†). Therefore, ZIF-8 encapsulation is essential to successfully carry out the nanospace confined carburization reaction to form $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites-embedded N-CNT assemblies. A closer observation on the edge of a $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assembly gives more details of the nanostructure. Fig. 3g clearly shows that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanoparticle is embedded in a carbon matrix. Furthermore, a highresolution TEM image reveals that the formed multi-walled NCNT is crystalline and the lattice fringes with an inter-planar distance of $0.36\ \mathsf{n m}$ correspond to the (002) plane (Fig. 3h). The crystalline nature of the carbon and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ materials is further confirmed by powder XRD analysis (Fig. S8, see $\mathsf{E S l^{\dagger}}$ ). The peak at about $26.2^{\circ}$ corresponds to the (002) plane of graphitic carbon, and the other peaks are attributed to $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ (JCPDS card no. 35-772). Different from $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies, two strong peaks at $44.7^{\circ}$ and $65.0^{\circ}$ in the XRD pattern of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-F e}/\mathsf{N}$ -CNT mixture derived from direct pyrolysis of MIL-88B are attributed to ɑ -Fe, indicating formation of large amount of metallic iron in the absence of ZIF-8 confinement. Although the carbon species near the asformed $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ particles in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assembly are partially graphitized (Fig. 3g,h), majority of the carbon matrix is determined to be amorphous as revealed by a large $\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{D}}/\mathsf{I}_{\mathsf{G}}$ ratio of 2.91 in the Raman spectrum (Fig. S9, see ESI†). Nitrogen sorption isotherms (Fig. S10, see $\mathsf{E S l^{+}}$ ) show a hysteresis loop, indicating the existence of mesopores. The surface area of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies is about 377 $\mathfrak{m}^{2}\{\mathfrak{g}^{-1}$ . EDX measurement confirms that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies contain C, N, and Fe elements (Fig. 3i). The atomic ratio of $\mathsf{F e/C}$ in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies is about 0.04. The absence of additional peaks from Zn element suggests the complete evaporation of Zn during the annealing process.35
The electrocatalytic activity of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies is first examined by cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements in 0.1 M KOH solution at room temperature. As shown in Fig. 4a, no obvious redox peak is observed for $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies in ${\sf N}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH solution. In contrast, when the solution is saturated with $\phantom{+}0_{2},$ a pronounced cathodic peak is clearly observed at $0.83\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs. RHE). Rotating disk electrode (RDE) measurements are further used to investigate the electrocatalytic activity and kinetics of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies in $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -saturated 0.1 M KOH solution (Fig. S11a, see ESI†). The corresponding Koutecky-Levich (K-L) plots (Fig. S11b, see ESI†) show the inverse of current density ${\mathfrak{G}}^{-1})$ as a function of the inverse of square root of rotating speed $(\omega^{-1/2})$ at different potentials from 0.4 to $0.7\:\mathsf{V}.$ . The number of electrons involved per ${\mathsf O}_{2}\left(n\right)$ in the ORR for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies is determined by the $k-L$ equation. The $\mathfrak{n}$ value is calculated to be in the range 3.95-3.98 for the potential range measured, suggesting that the ORR is dominated by the four-electron (4e) process and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ is directly reduced to $\mathsf{o}\mathsf{H}^{-}$ .36 The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}\cdot\mathsf{C N T}$ assemblies are benchmarked with a commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ electrocatalyst (20 wt.%; Johnson Matthey) for the ORR. Remarkably, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}\cdot\mathsf{C N T}$ sample exhibits higher ORR activity than the commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ electrocatalyst in terms of half-wave potential (0.85 V vs. 0.83 V; Fig. 4b). Compared with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ N-CNT assemblies, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ -Fe/N-CNT mixture consisting of aggregated large $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ particles and empty NCNTs shows a much lower half-wave potential of $0.75~\mathrm{~V~},$ probably due to serious aggregation of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites and detachment of catalytically active sites from the electron donating conductive matrix. Compared with $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}(\varpi|$ N-CNT assemblies and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C-F e/N\mathrm{-}C N T}$ mixture, the porous carbon nanoshells derived from ZIF-8 show more negative onset potential and lower diffusion current, indicating their inferior electrocatalytic activity for ORR. The better ORR activity of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies is further confirmed by a smaller Tafel slope ( $_{\sim78}$ versus ${\sim}116\ \mathsf{m v}$ decade-1 for ${\sf P t}/{\sf C},$ Fig. S12, see $\mathsf{E S l^{+}}$ ). Rotating ring-disk electrode (RRDE) measurement is carried out and the result is presented in Fig. S13 (see ESI†). In the potential range investigated, the yield of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ is below $1.3\%$ and the calculated electron transfer number ranges from
3.95 to 4.00, which is consistent with the result obtained from the K-L plots based on RDE results (Fig. S11).  The result further indicates that the excellent electrocatalytic activity has close relation with the structure of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies constructed from sufficiently separated small $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites in a hierarchically structured carbon matrix. The stability of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies, $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C-F e/N\mathrm{-}C N T}$ mixture, and commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst is investigated via chronoamperometric responses at $0.5\mathrm{V}$ in $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ -saturated $0.1\mathsf{M}$ KOH solution with the rotation speed of 1600 rpm for ${15000}s$ (Fig. 4c). After the stability test, $\sim94\%$ of the original current density is retained for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT electrode, whereas the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C-F e/N\mathrm{-}C N T}$ mixture and $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst electrodes show much higher current loss of $14\%$ and $24\%$ , respectively. The $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies and commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst are further compared by measuring the methanol crossover via chronoamperometric responses at the potential of $0.5\mathrm{V}$ in $\mathbf{O}_{2}.$ - saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte at the rotation speed of 1600 rpm (Fig. 4d). When methanol is added into the electrolyte, there is an instant drop in the current of the commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst electrode, while the current of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies electrode remains almost unchanged, indicating that the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N}$ -CNT assemblies possess excellent tolerance for methanol. All these results suggest that these F $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{e}}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{I}$ N-CNT assemblies have excellent electrocatalytic activity for ORR with high stability.
Compared with other MOFs-derived syntheses rVeiepwoArtiecled ,Otnlhinies approach employs a dual-MOFs compoDsOitI:e10.p1r0e3c9/uCrs6EoEr02w17it1Ah many Fe-based MIL-88B nanorods evenly embedded in each Zn-based ZIF-8 polyhedron host. In addition, the nanospace confined carburization process ensures homogeneous pyrolysis and efficiently prohibits excessive growth of electrocatalytically active $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites in a hierarchically structured carbon matrix.
Because of this unique structure, the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C@N-C N T}$ assemblies exhibit excellent electrocatalytic activity for ORR. In alkaline electrolyte, the performance is among the best when compared with numerous carbon-based noble metal-free electrocatalysts (Table S1, see ESI†). Considering the micrometer size of the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies, such electrocatalytic activity is very impressive. The high ORR performance of our $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{N-C N T}$ assemblies might be attributed to the following aspects. First, the sufficiently separated small $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites and graphitic N-CNTs as well as their porous carbon host render large active surface. Second, the thin graphitic carbon layer and porous structure are expected to facilitate the charge and mass transport within these particles, thus promoting the oxygen reduction process. Also, the carbon matrix might grant high robustness of the hierarchically structured particles and provide extra protection for the $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ nanocrystallites with high surface energy.
## Conclusions
In summary, a dual-MOFs confined-pyrolysis approach is developed to prepare iron carbide nanoparticles-embedded carbon nanotube assemblies. A key feature of this new strategy is that a Zn-base ZIF-8 polyhedron serves as the host for the effective encapsulation of many Fe-based MIL-88B nanorods. These MOF-in-MOF hybrid structures can be easily converted into a complex hierarchically structured electrocatalyst constructed from separated iron carbide nanocrystallites-embedded N-doped carbon nanotubes inside a porous N-doped carbon matrix. The as-prepared iron carbide nanoparticles-embedded carbon nanotube assemblies exhibit remarkable electrocatalytic activity and stability for oxygen reduction reaction compared with commercial Pt/C electrocatalyst. The superior electrocatalytic activity might be mainly attributed to the chemical composition and robust structure of the formed hierarchically structured hybrids. This dual-MOFs precursor strategy can be applied for the design and synthesis of many other hybrid functional materials and offers a new method for developing highly active MOFsderived electrocatalysts for different energy-related applications.
