Unique iron carbide nanocrystallites-embedded carbon nanotube assemblies prepared by a dual-MOFs confined-pyrolysis strategy exhibit superior electrocatalytic performance for oxygen reduction reaction.
## Broader Context
Efficient electrocatalysts for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) are of great importance for many electrochemical energy storage and conversion technologies including fuel cells and metal-air batteries. Replacing noble metal catalyst systems with high-performance and inexpensive ORR electrocatalysts is critical for the large-scale industrial applications. As an important class of porous materials with tunable structure and composition, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been considered as a promising materials platform for synthesizing various functional materials. In this work, we develop a dual-MOFs confined-pyrolysis strategy to prepare iron carbide nanoparticles-embedded N-doped carbon nanotube assemblies supported by a porous N-doped carbon matrix as efficient electrocatalysts for ORR. Of note, the introduction of the Fe-based MIL-88B guests into a Zn-based ZIF-8 host as co-precursor guarantees in situ homogeneously confined pyrolysis of MIL-88B nanorods and easy formation of small $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanocrystallites-embedded N-doped carbon nanotubes. Benefiting from the small size of these $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}$ nanocrystallites and the robust and porous N-doped carbon matrix, the as-prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathrm{C}}@\mathrm{N}$ -doped carbon nanotube assemblies exhibit remarkable electrocatalytic activity for ORR. This strategy can be applied for the design and synthesis of many other hybrid functional materials and offers a new method for developing highly active MOFs-derived electrocatalysts for different energy-related applications.
